<h2>The constitutional and procedural frame</h2>
<p>Three layers govern review in the Supreme Court. <strong>Article 137</strong> confers the power, &ldquo;subject to the provisions of any law made by Parliament or any rules made under Article 145.&rdquo; <strong>Order XLVII of the Supreme Court Rules, 2013</strong> supplies the procedure: the grounds, the thirty-day limitation, filing before the same Bench as far as practicable, and disposal by circulation. And the Court&rsquo;s settled practice supplies the temperament: review is an exception to finality, granted sparingly and only where the error is self-evident, not one that must be hunted down by a process of elaborate reasoning.</p>

<table class="law">
