  <tr><td>Mode of hearing</td><td>Circulation in chambers, without oral argument; open-court hearing in death sentence reviews</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Bench</td><td>The same judges, as far as practicable</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What counts as an error apparent</h2>
<p>The phrase does most of the work in review practice, and it is narrower than litigants hope. An error apparent on the face of the record is one that leaps from the record without long debate: the Court proceeded on a statutory provision that had been repealed, overlooked a binding precedent squarely cited, recorded a concession never made, or decided the matter on a factual premise contradicted by the record itself. What does <em>not</em> qualify is equally well settled: a different possible view of the evidence, a fresh argument that could have been raised earlier but was not, dissatisfaction with the weight given to a contention, or the hope that a differently-composed Bench might think differently. A review petition drafted as a better version of the dismissed appeal is the surest candidate for dismissal by circulation, often with a single line.</p>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Review is</strong><br>Correction of a demonstrable, self-evident error; a response to genuinely new material that diligence could not have produced earlier.</div>
  <div class="arrow">≠</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Review is not</strong><br>A rehearing, a second appeal in disguise, a chance to re-argue what was considered, or a forum for points deliberately held back.</div>
</div>

<h2>How the petition moves</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Drafting.</strong> The petition must state the grounds with precision and is accompanied by a certificate of counsel that it is the first review and that the grounds fall within the Rules — a discipline the Registry enforces at filing.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Filing within thirty days.</strong> Any delay needs a condonation application with real explanation; limitation in review matters is applied strictly.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Circulation.</strong> The petition is placed before the same Bench, as far as practicable, and considered in chambers on the papers. There is no appearance, no oral submission, no mentioning for an early date in the ordinary course.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Outcome.</strong> Most petitions are dismissed by a short order recording that no error apparent is made out. Where the Court finds substance, it may issue notice, direct an open-court hearing, and ultimately recall or modify the judgment.</div>
</div>

<h2>The death sentence exception, and what lies beyond</h2>
<p>Because the ordinary mode of disposal is on paper, the Court has carved out one humane exception: a petitioner seeking review of a judgment imposing the <strong>death sentence</strong> is entitled to an oral hearing in open court before a Bench of adequate strength. The irreversibility of the penalty justifies the departure from chambers practice.</p>
<p>When review fails, the judgment is final. The <strong>curative petition</strong> remains as an extraordinary, last-resort jurisdiction — confined to grounds such as breach of natural justice or a judge&rsquo;s undisclosed interest, requiring certification by a Senior Advocate, and circulated to the senior-most judges of the Court. It is not a third round of merits; it exists to prevent abuse of process and gross miscarriage of justice, and relief under it is rare enough to be newsworthy when granted.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p>Decide on review immediately after judgment — the thirty-day clock and the need for the same Bench both reward speed.</p>
  <p>Anchor every ground to the record: page, paragraph, and the precise overlooked provision or authority.</p>
  <p>Resist the temptation to re-argue; two tight grounds outperform twenty recycled ones.</p>
  <p>Advise clients candidly on the statistics: review is designed to fail in the ordinary case, and its real function is to catch the extraordinary one.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">Review petitions in the High Courts follow a related but distinct regime — Order XLVII of the Code of Civil Procedure on the civil side, with its own body of practice. The present note is confined to the Supreme Court.</div>
